  "worked_solution": "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=(x-2)^{2}{e}^{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =2(x-2){e}^{x}+(x-2)^{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =(x-2){e}^{x}[2+x-2] \\\\  & =x(x-2){e}^{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nx(x-2){e}^{x}=0\\,\u27f9\\,x=0,\\,2.\n$$\n\nSince $x=2$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,1]$, the critical points are $x=0$ and the endpoints $x=-1,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =(-1-2)^{2}{e}^{-1}=\\frac{9}{e}\u22483.31 \\\\ f(0) & =(0-2)^{2}{e}^{0}=4 \\\\ f(1) & =(1-2)^{2}{e}^{1}=e\u22482.72\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(0)=4,$ so the global maximum is $4.$\n\n- The least value is $f(1)=e,$ so the global minimum is $e.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061690/assets/adeb7eb92f270a216f30cd0466e91d0363c5a50b17053316e6e9f44d0dfaaf23.png)",
